caymanslm: widen the QCOM watchdog bark window 16s -> 30s

The APPS watchdog (drivers/soc/qcom/watchdog_v2.c:wdog_bark_handler) responds to
a bark by immediately calling msm_trigger_wdog_bite() -- a hardware SoC reset --
with panic() only as a fallback. So a kernel *hang* (a spinning hook / deadlock)
becomes a hard reset with nothing captured: at the stock 16s bark the hardware
bite fires before anything can be read out.

The warn-only soft-lockup / hung-task detectors (config/diag.fragment) print the
stuck backtrace to the kernel ring at ~20s (2 x watchdog_thresh) while the other
CPUs keep running -- but only if they get to run before the bite. Widening
bark-time to 30s gives them that window, so root can read the trace live with
`dmesg` before the reset. On this device that live read is the only capture that
works: the LG bootloader wipes the ramoops/pstore RAM on every reset, so nothing
survives a reboot.

This does NOT fix the com.eltavine.duckdetector reboot that first motivated it:
that turned out to be a genuine panic() which reboots regardless, not a hang the
detectors catch (see tests/baselines/duckdetector-kernel-panic-UPSTREAM-REPORT.md).
It is kept as a general aid for debugging future hook hangs. Cost: a genuine
unrecoverable hang resets 14s later than stock.

